/**
 * The crowds: every catalogued Centaur, the Neptune Trojans, and the
 * kilometre-class near-Earth asteroids in their four families -- as element
 * sets, one row per body, for `populationClouds.js` to fly on the GPU.
 *
 * `docs/populations-not-yet-built.md` sections 1, 2 and 4: "the biggest
 * empty space in the scene" (between Jupiter and Neptune, where only the
 * eight named Centaurs were drawn), the near-Earth objects as a population
 * rather than six individuals, and Neptune's Trojans. The owner, final
 * round: "do all buddy everything at once".
 *
 * ## Unlike Jupiter's swarms, this is a catalogue
 *
 * `resonantSwarms.js` had to draw Jupiter's Trojans as a statistical picture
 * because the MPC lists could not be downloaded where it was built. These
 * could: every row below is one real object's osculating elements from the
 * JPL Small-Body Database query API (`sbdb_query.api`), queried 8 October
 * 2026. No point is invented.
 *
 *   CENTAUR_ELEMENTS   SBDB orbit class CEN -- JPL's definition, 5.5 AU <
 *                      a < 30.1 AU. 1,056 objects on the query date; the
 *                      ten of them that are Neptune Trojans (a ~30 AU) are
 *                      moved to NEPTUNE_TROJANS, and the eight drawn
 *                      individually (Chiron, Pholus, Chariklo, Echeclus,
 *                      Nessus, Asbolus, Hylonome, Bienor) are left out so
 *                      no point sits on top of a body: 1,038 rows.
 *                      Two of those are Uranus's Trojans, (636872)
 *                      2014 YX49 and (687170) 2011 QF99, which JPL files
 *                      as Centaurs; round 37 moved them to PLANET_TROJANS
 *                      so they ride in Uranus's frame: 1,036 rows.
 *   ATIRA_ELEMENTS     class IEO, a < 1 AU and aphelion Q < 0.983 AU --
 *                      orbits entirely inside Earth's. All 38 known.
 *   ATEN_ELEMENTS      class ATE, a < 1 AU, Q > 0.983 AU.
 *   APOLLO_ELEMENTS    class APO, a > 1 AU, perihelion q < 1.017 AU.
 *   AMOR_ELEMENTS      class AMO, 1.017 < q < 1.3 AU -- they approach and
 *                      never cross.
 *                      These three: every near-Earth asteroid with absolute
 *                      magnitude H < 17.75, the usual stand-in for "about a
 *                      kilometre or larger" at typical albedo. 867 objects on
 *                      the query date (30 Atens, 427 Apollos, 396 Amors once
 *                      the drawn ones -- Eros, Phaethon, Toutatis, Cruithne
 *                      and the rest -- are left out). Of all 42,590 known
 *                      near-Earth asteroids, 11,628 have H < 22 (~140 m);
 *                      the scene draws the big ones, which are the ones that
 *                      matter for impacts and are nearly all found.
 *   NEPTUNE_TROJANS    every body with 29.6 < a < 30.6 AU and e < 0.3 whose
 *                      mean longitude at its element epoch is 25-95 degrees
 *                      ahead of Neptune (L4) or behind it (L5); Neptune's
 *                      from Standish's Table 1 elements. 31 at L4 and 10 at
 *                      L5, plus 2004 KV18 at -102 degrees, a known L5
 *                      Trojan on a wide libration (Horner & Lykawka 2012).
 *                      The test turned away five others in the band --
 *                      among them (309239) 2007 RW10, which sits 10 degrees
 *                      from Neptune as its quasi-satellite.
 *
 * Each row is [a AU, e, i deg, node deg, arg. perihelion deg, mean anomaly
 * deg, H mag (-1 where SBDB has none), epoch JD]; the epoch is left off
 * when it is JD 2461200.5 (9 June 2026), SBDB's standard epoch, which most
 * share. Short-arc objects keep their own older epoch and are propagated
 * from it -- their positions today are uncertain by much more than a
 * drawn point, and nothing here pretends otherwise.
 *
 * Precision: values were transferred at four significant figures (a, e) and
 * two decimals (angles) -- about 0.01 degree, invisible at any framing the
 * scene has. Checked on receipt: all 867 near-Earth rows sit in the family
 * their own a and e put them in (no mismatches), and every Centaur row has
 * 5.5 < a < 30.2 and 0 <= e < 1.
 */

